Target systems
==============

The main development work of the Geneva library collection is currently done
on Ubuntu Linux. Geneva is expected to compile on most reasonably recent Linux
distributions, provided a new enough compiler is available (see "Prerequisites"
below). Linux is the only regularly tested platform at this time.

The recommended system is Ubuntu 26.10. Its own package archive carries a CUDA
toolkit and an NVIDIA driver recent enough for Geneva's optional GPU features
(see "GPU support on Ubuntu 26.10" below), so no vendor repository is needed.
Without a GPU, any Linux distribution that meets the prerequisites will do.

All of the setup described in this file can also be left to a container: the
image definition in containers/geneva/ builds Boost and Geneva inside Ubuntu
26.10 with Podman or Docker, so the host needs nothing but a container engine.
containers/geneva/README.md explains how to build and use it.

Geneva has also been built experimentally on macOS using the bundled Clang
compiler, and much older versions were known to compile under Windows with the
Microsoft Visual C++ compiler. Neither is covered by regular testing today, so
your mileage may vary. Broader, regularly tested platform support is planned for
the future.

The authors would very much appreciate your feedback through the Geneva GitHub
project. In case you encounter any bugs or issues please let us know through the
Geneva bugtracker: https://github.com/gemfony/geneva-suite/issues .

For further information, please also have a look at our user manual, available
from the Downloads section of our web page http://www.gemfony.eu .


Hardware
========

Geneva is a 64-bit code base and requires a 64-bit processor of one of the two
mainstream families:

 - x86-64 (any modern Intel- or AMD-compatible 64-bit CPU), or
 - AArch64 (64-bit ARM).

Both are first-class, regularly tested targets. Two hardware features are used
when present but are never required:

 - SIMD: the random-number engine has a vectorised bulk-refill path (AVX2 on
   x86-64, NEON on AArch64), detected automatically at compile time, with a
   scalar fallback otherwise.
 - A CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU: enables the optional GPU consumer and an optional
   CUDA random-number backend. Without a usable CUDA toolkit the random numbers
   come from the CPU (SIMD where available) and the GPU consumer is not built, so
   evaluations run on the CPU consumers.


Prerequisites
=============

 - CMake, version 4.0 or later (the build system). Ubuntu 26.10 ships 4.3.
   Where the system's CMake is older, take a current one from PyPI
   ("python3 -m pip install cmake", e.g. in a virtual environment) or from
   Kitware (binaries on https://cmake.org/download/, Debian/Ubuntu packages
   from https://apt.kitware.com/).

 - A C++23 compiler: the GNU Compiler Collection (g++) version 15 or later, or
   Clang version 22 or later -- the versions Ubuntu 26.10 ships.

 - The Boost libraries (see http://www.boost.org), version 1.91 or newer. Boost
   should be compiled from source and installed for the system, in
   C++23 mode (see "Compiling Boost on Linux" below). The
   distribution's Boost packages are frequently older than required and/or not
   built in C++23 mode, and are therefore not recommended.

 - Catch2, version 3, but only when building the test code.

 - The Git version control tool, only if you build from the version-control
   tree rather than from a released tarball.

 - A Geneva build must know the commit of its sources: a server of the
   networked consumers and its clients must be built from the same one. It is
   taken from git when the sources are a git checkout (with "-dirty" appended
   when a tracked file has changed), else from the file GEM_COMMIT at the top
   of the sources, which "git archive" fills in. Sources copied without .git
   need the id written into that file (the container build takes it as the
   build argument GEM_COMMIT); without it the configure stops and says so.

Optional:

 - A CUDA toolkit, for the GPU consumer and/or the CUDA random-number backend.
 - An MPI implementation, for the MPI consumer.
 - An MQTT broker, for the MQTT consumer (built when the Boost in use has
   Boost.MQTT5, which is part of every Boost Geneva accepts; BUILDMQTTCONSUMER in
   genevaConfig.gcfg). Its tests start a mosquitto of their own (Debian/Ubuntu:
   the package mosquitto) and are registered when configure finds it.
 - A plotting viewer -- ROOT (http://root.cern), gnuplot, matplotlib or octave --
   to render the plot scripts that some examples emit. None of these is required to
   run Geneva or to produce the scripts, only to view them.


Installation prerequisites on Debian/Ubuntu
-------------------------------------------

The compiler and tools come from the distribution; Boost is compiled separately
(see "Compiling Boost on Linux" below):

sudo apt-get install --no-install-recommends cmake git g++ clang catch2

The Boost package of Ubuntu 26.10 (libboost-dev) is version 1.90, older than the
required 1.91, which is one more reason to compile Boost yourself.

The optional components, as far as the distribution provides them -- an MPI
implementation for the MPI consumer, and plot viewers for the scripts the
examples emit:

sudo apt-get install --no-install-recommends libopenmpi-dev openmpi-bin
sudo apt-get install --no-install-recommends gnuplot octave python3-matplotlib

All of this -- the packages, the optional components, and Boost compiled as
described in "Compiling Boost on Linux" below -- can also be installed with the
Ansible playbook scripts/ansible/geneva-host.yml, on this machine or on others:

ansible-playbook -K -i localhost, -c local scripts/ansible/geneva-host.yml

Its header lists the choices it takes (the Boost version and compiler, MPI, the
plot viewers, the MQTT broker, CUDA, and a host prepared as the server of a
distributed measurement).


GPU support on Ubuntu 26.10
---------------------------

The GPU consumer and the CUDA random-number backend need CUDA 13.3 or newer, and
the build checks this twice: the CUDA toolkit must be at least 13.3, and so must
the CUDA version the installed NVIDIA driver supports. If either is older, the
configure output says which. With the GPU switches at their default ("auto")
CUDA is then switched off and the build continues without it; with a switch set
to "on", configuring stops with an error.

Ubuntu 26.10 provides both parts from its own archive. Two packages look like
the obvious choice and are not: the driver that "ubuntu-drivers" marks as
recommended (nvidia-driver-580-open) supports only CUDA 13.0, and the package
nvidia-cuda-toolkit contains CUDA 12.4. Install these instead:

sudo apt-get install nvidia-driver-615-open cuda-toolkit-13-3

Reboot after the driver change, then check what the driver supports:

nvidia-smi -q | grep CUDA

The toolkit lands in /usr/local/cuda-13.3, reachable as /usr/local/cuda, where
the build finds it without further settings.


The broker of the MQTT consumer
-------------------------------

The MQTT consumer (--consumer mqtt) needs an MQTT 5 broker that its server and
its clients reach: mosquitto 2 or any other broker that speaks MQTT 5. Geneva
starts none; --mqtt_ip and --mqtt_port name it (default 127.0.0.1:1883).

 - The broker must send small packets at once. In mosquitto that is
   "set_tcp_nodelay true" (its default is false). Without it every second small
   message waits about 40 ms for the receiver's delayed acknowledgement: with 8
   clients and evaluations of 10 ms the speed-up was 1.6 instead of websocket's
   6.0. With it, 402 clients reached 203 (websocket 286) at 10 ms and the same as
   websocket from about 1 s on.
 - Server and clients connect anonymously and without encryption, as the
   websocket consumer does: run the broker on a trusted network only.
 - A run with N clients takes 2N+1 broker connections: one of each client, one
   of the server for each client, and the server's own for the joins. The 1024
   open files a broker started by systemd may have suffice for about 500 clients;
   raise its limit for more.
 - Runs that share a broker use different topic prefixes (--mqtt_topic): a
   server takes every client that joins under its prefix.
 - A work item or a result travels as one MQTT packet of at most 2,000,000 bytes
   (mosquitto 2.1's default limit, and the fixed limit of every end); a larger
   one fails. Use the websocket or the MPI consumer for larger items.

A mosquitto configuration for one machine (where an AppArmor profile confines
mosquitto, as on Ubuntu, it reads one only from /etc/mosquitto):

listener 18830 127.0.0.1
allow_anonymous true
set_tcp_nodelay true
max_connections -1
persistence false

Start it with "mosquitto -c <that file>" and pass --mqtt_port 18830 to the server
and its clients.


Building and installing Geneva
==============================

Geneva REQUIRES an out-of-source build: configure and compile in a directory
that is separate from the source checkout. The convenience script enforces this
and refuses to run inside the source tree. Use one of the two procedures below.


a) Using prepareBuild.sh and a configuration file
-------------------------------------------------

Geneva comes with a script that wraps the somewhat involved cmake call behind a
configuration file, so the chosen options can be recorded once and reused.
With GENEVAHOME naming the Geneva checkout (export GENEVAHOME=$HOME/geneva, for
instance), create a build directory, copy the template config over and adapt it:

mkdir -p $HOME/build && cd $HOME/build
cp $GENEVAHOME/scripts/genevaConfig.gcfg .
# ... edit genevaConfig.gcfg (compiler, BOOSTROOT, build mode, install dir,
#     and the optional MPI/GPU/CUDA features) ...

Then configure (and optionally build) by handing the file to the script:

$GENEVAHOME/scripts/prepareBuild.sh ./genevaConfig.gcfg --clean --build
sudo make install   # if installing into a system directory

Useful script options:

  --clean             return the build directory to a pristine state before
                      configuring
  --build             compile immediately after configuring, using all cores
                      (make -j$(nproc))
  --dryrun            print the exact cmake command that would be run, without
                      running it (handy for inspection or for an IDE)
  --generate-preset   write a CMakeUserPresets.json encoding the .gcfg settings,
                      for CLion or VS Code to pick up
  -y, --yes           assume "yes" for the interactive prompts
  --help, -h          show full usage


b) Direct call to cmake
-----------------------

Everything the script does reduces to a cmake invocation, which can be issued by
hand. From the build directory, point cmake at the source tree:

cd $HOME/build
cmake $GENEVAHOME -DGEM_BUILD_TYPE="Release"
make -j$(nproc)
sudo make install

To choose a custom installation directory (the default is /opt/geneva):

cmake $GENEVAHOME -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="/where/the/library/goes"

The configuration variables map onto the .gcfg settings: INSTALLDIR becomes
CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX, BUILDMODE becomes GEM_BUILD_TYPE (Release, Debug,
RelWithDebInfo, MinSizeRel or Sanitize), BUILDTESTCODE becomes
GEM_BUILD_TESTS, and so on; the compiler is selected with CMAKE_C_COMPILER /
CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER. The simplest way to see the full command for a given
configuration is to run "prepareBuild.sh ./my.gcfg --dryrun".

If Boost was installed in a non-standard location (for instance /opt/boost),
make it known to cmake either by its prefix:

cmake $GENEVAHOME -DBOOST_ROOT="/opt/boost"

or, if headers and libraries do not share a prefix, by both locations:

cmake $GENEVAHOME -DBOOST_INCLUDEDIR="/opt/boost/include" \
                   -DBOOST_LIBRARYDIR="/opt/boost/lib"

Adding -DCMAKE_VERBOSE_MAKEFILE="1" shows the compiler command lines.


Running the test suite
======================

A build with the test code enabled (BUILDTESTCODE in the .gcfg,
GEM_BUILD_TESTS in cmake -- both default to on) registers the entire suite
with CTest. Run it from the build directory:

cd $HOME/build
ctest

The suite runs in parallel; give CTest as many jobs as you have cores:

ctest -j$(nproc)

Every test is always registered; none is hidden behind a switch. What decides
whether a given run includes a test is its SIZE -- one label per test, chosen
from its measured runtime (with the one exception named below), so the choice
is made when the tests are run rather than when the tree is configured:

  S    under 1 second
  M    1 to 10 seconds
  L    10 to 60 seconds
  XL   over a minute

Every registered test carries exactly one of these. The three commands worth
remembering:

ctest -j$(nproc) -LE "L|XL"     while changing code -- seconds
ctest -j$(nproc) -LE XL         before a commit, and before a push
ctest -j$(nproc) -L XL          once per larger piece of work, and not before
                                a push -- it is a rot check, not a gate

Tests also carry descriptive labels, which are informational only and never
decide whether a test runs: the owning library (common, hap, weft, dietrich,
courtier, geneva), "example" for the end-to-end runs of libs/*/examples/,
and "benchmark" for the runs of the programs whose whole purpose is a
convergence- or throughput-scale measurement and for the checks of their tools.
The longest of them (GOptimizationBenchmark, GParallelisationOverhead with its
reference run and, in a GPU-consumer build, GCUDAOptBenchmark) are XL, but it
is their size that keeps them out of a commit gate, not their name -- a
benchmark turned down to ten seconds would be an L and would gate like any
other L. The exception are the three convergence shards of GenevaStandardTests
(ea-highdim10k, ea-highdim, gsa): they are XL because they are convergence-scale
runs (Invariant 22 in DEVELOPMENT-INVARIANTS.md), not because of their time,
and run before a release rather than in the commit gate.

Adding a test? Measure it and give it a size. Run it once on an otherwise idle
machine with "ctest -j1 -R <name>" -- serial, because under -j a test's reported
time includes whatever it was competing with -- and pass the letter to the
registration:

  GEM_TEST_SIZE(<test-name> <S|M|L|XL>)          for a plain ADD_TEST
  gem_<library>_test(<name> <size> <sources>)       for the per-library helpers

A test within about 20 % of a boundary is rounded UP, so a borderline case
lands in the more expensive class rather than sneaking into a fast loop. A size
that turns out to be wrong is an ordinary review fix: correct the letter, do not
work around it. Sizes are re-measured when a test's cost genuinely changes.

A handful of tests are registered only when what they need was actually found at
configure time -- the two MPI examples need an MPI launcher, example 11 needs a
Python 3 interpreter, and the GPU examples and the GPU benchmark exist only in a
build with the GPU consumer enabled. Configure says so for each one it leaves
out, so a missing test always has a stated reason.


Finding the libraries at run time on Linux
==========================================

Geneva's installed libraries, modules and programs (examples, benchmarks,
tests) carry run paths: they find Geneva's libraries relative to their own
location, also after an installation of the default layout (no INSTALL_PREFIX_*
set, see below) has been moved, and they find a Boost outside the system's
library directories -- one compiled by yourself, for example -- in the
directory it was linked from when Geneva was built. After "make install"
nothing needs to be set for them. If that Boost is moved or removed later, they
no longer find it.

A program of your own that is built with CMake and find_package(Geneva) gets a
run path to Geneva's and Boost's library directories in its build tree and runs
there as it is. "make install" of your project removes that run path (CMake's
default). To keep it, configure your project with

  cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH_USE_LINK_PATH=ON ...

A program linked by hand with -L and -l has no run path unless you give it
one, for example

  g++ ... -L/opt/geneva/lib -lgemfony-geneva -Wl,-rpath,/opt/geneva/lib

Geneva's libraries then find what they need themselves; add Boost's library
directory in the same way if your program links Boost libraries itself.

Instead of a run path, the library directories (Geneva's, and Boost's if you
compiled it yourself) can be made known to the dynamic linker, system-wide
with a file /etc/ld.so.conf.d/geneva.conf that names them, one per line,
followed by "sudo ldconfig", or per session:

export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/opt/geneva/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH"


Consuming Geneva from your own project
======================================

An installed Geneva is best consumed through its CMake package:

  find_package(Geneva CONFIG REQUIRED)
  target_link_libraries(myTarget PRIVATE Geneva::geneva)

The imported targets are Geneva::common, Geneva::hap, Geneva::weft,
Geneva::dietrich, Geneva::courtier and Geneva::geneva; linking Geneva::geneva
pulls in the layers beneath it, the C++23
requirement and the Boost dependency transitively.

An installation made without the separate INSTALL_PREFIX_* locations (the
default) is relocatable: everything in it refers to the installation's own
location, so it may be moved, installed with "cmake --install --prefix", or
staged with DESTDIR and used from there. With the four INSTALL_PREFIX_*
variables set, the exported targets name those absolute directories.

libs/geneva/quickstart/ is a working project of exactly this shape and the
recommended starting point: it is not built by the in-tree build, it locates
Geneva only through the installed package, and it can be copied out of the tree
as-is. Its README has the step-by-step standalone build.

The package also brings GEM_STAMP_PROGRAM(<target> [IDENTITY <text>]), which
stamps a program with the commit of your project's sources (taken from git at
every build when the project is inside a git work tree, at its top or in a
subdirectory; "-dirty" when a tracked file of that repository has changed) or
with the text you give. A server
of the networked consumers names it to its clients, and a client built from
other sources ends; docs/writing-optimization-problems.md says more.


Recommended software
====================

A Geneva optimization can emit ready-to-run plotting scripts for several backends
-- ROOT (see http://root.cern, a C++ analysis framework widely used in particle
physics), gnuplot, matplotlib or octave. Installing the viewer for whichever
backend you prefer lets you render the figures the examples emit. None of these is
required to run Geneva or to produce the scripts, only to view the results.


Compiling Boost on Linux
========================

Download a Boost release, unpack it, and configure the build with bootstrap.sh,
naming the installation prefix (here /opt/boost):

wget https://archives.boost.io/release/1.92.0/source/boost_1_92_0.tar.gz
tar xzf boost_1_92_0.tar.gz
cd boost_1_92_0
./bootstrap.sh --prefix=/opt/boost

Then compile and install with b2. The decisive flag is cxxflags="-std=c++23":
Boost must be built in the same C++23 mode as Geneva. Installing into /opt
requires root:

sudo ./b2 -j$(nproc) \
   cxxflags="-std=c++23" \
   variant=release \
   link=shared \
   threading=multi \
   boost.process.fs=std \
   install

boost.process.fs=std is needed as well: Geneva starts external programs through
Boost.Process, and with this setting Boost.Process uses std::filesystem instead
of Boost.Filesystem, which Geneva does not use otherwise. A Boost built without
it does not link with Geneva (the libraries disagree about the path type).

To build only the components Geneva uses, add:
   --with-atomic --with-json --with-program_options --with-process --with-regex
To match Geneva's compiler too, pass "--with-toolset=clang" to bootstrap.sh and
"toolset=clang" to b2.

Afterwards, point genevaConfig.gcfg (BOOSTROOT="/opt/boost") or the cmake call
(-DBOOST_ROOT="/opt/boost") at this location. Geneva's libraries and programs
find this Boost through their run paths; for programs of your own see "Finding
the libraries at run time on Linux" above.
